window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('d1',{"s":["<section id=\"d1-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · 2020–2025</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Two ways to stop partners inventing private conventions</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Self-play (Tesauro 1994)</b> · Agents trained against themselves score high but invent private conventions that fail with new partners.</li><li><b>Cooperative MARL (SAD)</b> · Hanabi agents trained and scored together; SAD (Hu &amp; Foerster 2019) was the Hanabi state of the art.</li><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · Adapt to a partner from observed behaviour; zero-shot gives no partner data at all.</li><li><b>Cognitive hierarchies</b> · Camerer et al.: each level best-responds to the one below; OBL borrows the idea of levels.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Two agents are trained separately, then meet once. Self-play conventions are arbitrary, so cross-play with a stranger fails.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Block arbitrary conventions: relabel the partner by game symmetries (Other-Play), or re-read the past through a fixed policy (OBL).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/d1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Solid arrows: method, code or agents reused. Comparison-only links are left out.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Zero-shot coordination</b> · play well with a new partner on the first try</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Cross-play (XP)</b> · score of agents from different training runs</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Symmetry</b> · a relabeling that leaves the game unchanged</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Belief</b> · a learned guess of the hidden state given history</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief assume cooperative, simulated games</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#54237B\">Cooperative, shared reward</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">One shared reward. Hanabi is the main testbed; the formalism paper uses only toy lever games.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#54237B\">Separate training, one meeting</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Partners are built independently and meet at test time; Conventions instead adapts to partners it trained with.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#54237B\">Symmetries given, then learned</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Other-Play and Equivariant ZSC are handed the symmetries; ER symmetries (2025) learns them from a policy pool.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#54237B\">Environment access</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">OBL needs environment access (per Equivariant ZSC); Gen. beliefs rolls out games with pool policies.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#54237B\">Partners built for ZSC</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Equivariant ZSC assumes partners also aim for ZSC; Gen. beliefs also tests partners not trained for it (SAD).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Self-play MARL (SAD), domain randomization, cognitive hierarchies, R2D2 agents and learned belief models.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#54237B\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Other-Play and OBL made Hanabi cross-play work; ER symmetries learns the symmetries; Gen. beliefs helps with unseen partners.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A symmetry branch and a belief branch, with cross-links</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Symmetry methods prune equivalent conventions; belief methods avoid them by reading the past as fixed; later papers patch or reuse both.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/d1_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:470px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Symmetry branch</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2020–2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Other-Play</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ZSC formalism</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Equivariant ZSC</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ER sym.</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Other-Play trains vs relabeled partners; later work adds tie-breaking, equivariant nets, then learned symmetries.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#54237B\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Belief branch</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">OBL</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Gen. beliefs</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">OBL reads the past as a fixed policy pi0; Gen. beliefs learns one Transformer belief over many policies and uses it for search.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#54237B\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Cross-links, side line</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Conventions</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Equivariant ZSC</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#EEE3F7;border:2px solid #54237B;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Gen. beliefs</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Equivariant ZSC symmetrizes OBL agents; Gen. beliefs uses OP pools; Conventions (2021) learns partner modules, with no solid edge.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Other-Play 2020 · 1 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Other-play: coordinate with partners never met</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on self-play MARL, mainly SAD (Hu &amp; Foerster 2019), plus domain randomization; no earlier member of this tree.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>The game symmetries are given</li><li>Separate training, one meeting</li><li>Partners are zero-shot agents too</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Trains each agent against a randomly relabeled copy of its partner (a symmetry such as a card-color permutation).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ZSC formalism, Equivariant ZSC and ER symmetries build on it; OBL and Gen. beliefs reuse its setting and agents.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi cross-play 2.52 -&gt; 15.32 (SAD vs SAD+OP, 2 players, max 25); 20 humans 15.75 vs 9.15.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">“Other-Play” for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2020</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · OBL 2021 · 2 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Off-belief learning: no shared conventions needed</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Adopts Other-Play&#x27;s ZSC setting and uses OP agents as baseline and partners; its own method comes from cognitive hierarchies, R2D2 and learned beliefs.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Turn-based cooperative Dec-POMDP</li><li>A fixed starting policy pi0</li><li>A simulator and a learned belief model</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reads the past as if a fixed policy pi0 played it, plans the future with its own policy pi1; repeat for levels 2, 3, 4.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Gen. beliefs reuses its code and belief idea; Equivariant ZSC symmetrizes its agents; ER symmetries only compares.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi cross-play 23.76 (level 4) vs 21.77 for Other-Play (2 players, max 25).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Off-Belief Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · ZSC formalism 2021 · 3 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Other-play is not optimal; tie-breaking repairs it</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Other-Play: formalizes its label-free setting and adds tie-breaking to fix its suboptimality.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Players share an algorithm, not labels</li><li>Finite cooperative Dec-POMDP</li><li>A pre-agreed tie-breaking function</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Defines the label-free coordination problem, shows Other-Play can fail, and picks one policy by a shared random-network score.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ER symmetries borrows its three-lever game idea and takes inspiration from its symmetrizer.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Two-stage lever game: OP about 0, tie-breaking about 0.5 (the optimum); asymmetric game about 0.9 -&gt; 1.97 of 2.0.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">A New Formalism, Method and Open Issues for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Treutlein et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Conventions 2021 · 4 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learn rules and conventions apart to adapt fast</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Lewis&#x27;s idea of a convention and modular policy nets (Devin et al.); cites Other-Play only as a contrast. No solid edge in this tree.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Rational partners, each with a convention</li><li>Known task and partner identities</li><li>Tasks differ only in reward</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A task module (rules) times one partner module per partner (conventions), with a regularizer pulling the task module to the partner average.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Gen. beliefs and Equivariant ZSC cite it as a contrast (bandits and 1-colour Hanabi only); no method reused.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Task module within 0.01 of the true marginals vs 1.15 for BaselineAgg (Arms 3 bandit, lower is better).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">On the Critical Role of Conventions in Adaptive Human-AI Collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Shih et al. · ICLR 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Gen. beliefs 2022 · 5 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A belief over many policies reads new partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses OBL&#x27;s codebase and learned-belief idea, and Other-Play&#x27;s SAD and OP policy pools; contrasts itself with Conventions and Other-Play&#x27;s symmetry route.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>A pool of pre-trained partner policies</li><li>Hidden state splits into features (own cards)</li><li>A simulator for search at test time</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A Transformer belief trained on rollouts of many policies, used for Monte Carlo search or fed to a best-response policy.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Later members only cite it; none builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi (2p, max 25) vs 7 unseen SAD partners: best response 10.29 -&gt; 12.36 with the generalized belief.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Generalized Beliefs for Cooperative AI</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Muglich et al. · ICML 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Equivariant ZSC 2022 · 6 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Equivariant networks stop symmetry breaking in ZSC</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Other-Play: replaces its data-augmentation symmetry handling with equivariant nets (symmetrizer from MDP homomorphic nets); also applies it to OBL agents.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Some environment symmetries are known</li><li>Partners are also built for ZSC</li><li>A small symmetry group, linear first/last layers</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Averages a network over relabeled inputs and outputs of a symmetry group, so the policy is equivariant by construction.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ER symmetries reuses the symmetrizer as a test-time operator over learned symmetries.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi cross-play 16.08 vs 15.32 for Other-Play; bombouts 19.7% vs 30.8% (2022, max 25).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Equivariant Networks for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Muglich et al. · NeurIPS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-s07\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · ER symmetries 2025 · 7 of 7</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learned return symmetries help strangers coordinate</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Other-Play (ER symmetries replace its symmetry group); reuses the Equivariant ZSC symmetrizer and a ZSC formalism game idea; only compares with OBL.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Cooperative game, independent partners</li><li>No known symmetries or dynamics model</li><li>A pool of self-play-optimal policies</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Learns relabelings of actions and observations that keep expected return across a policy pool, then runs other-play with them.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d1_s07.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi cross-play 21.64 vs 8.61 for other-play with all 120 known symmetries (IPPO 4.02; max 25).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Expected Return Symmetries</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Muglich et al. · ICLR 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Hanabi cross-play improves, but benchmarks differ</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Mostly 2-player Hanabi cross-play (max 25) between independent agents; Conventions uses bandit and block tasks.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Baselines are mostly earlier members (SAD, Other-Play, OBL); ZSC formalism and ER symmetries add toy games.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Human-facing: only Other-Play tests 20 people; OBL uses a human-data proxy bot (Clone Bot).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EEE3F7;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Different benchmarks, bases and partners: do not rank rows. Read each as a gain over its own baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Other-Play</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p, cross-play</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">SAD 2.52 -&gt; SAD+OP 15.32</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">OBL</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p, cross-play</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Level 4 23.76 vs Other-play 21.77</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">ZSC formalism</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Two-stage lever game (toy)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">OP about 0; tie-breaking about 0.5 (optimum)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Conventions</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Arms 3 bandit, distance to marginals</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">0.01 vs 1.15 for BaselineAgg (lower is better)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Gen. beliefs</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p, 7 unseen SAD partners</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Best response 10.29 -&gt; 12.36 with the belief</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">Equivariant ZSC</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi cross-play</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">D10 16.08 vs OP 15.32; bombouts 19.7% vs 30.8%</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#54237B\">ER symmetries</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi cross-play (IPPO)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">21.64 vs 8.61 (OP, known symmetries)</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d1-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#54237B\">Symmetry &amp; belief · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #54237B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#54237B;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Symmetries are relabelings that must be given or learned; the two-lever game still fails (ER symmetries).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #54237B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#54237B;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Cross-play with AI partners is a proxy; the one human study here has 20 players, called preliminary.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #54237B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#54237B;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Gen. beliefs gains are modest and Hanabi-only; OBL needs environment access.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Symmetry and belief</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">7 papers · 2020–2025 · Other-Play → OBL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Tree d1, 7 members: Other-Play (2020), OBL (2021), ZSC formalism (2021), Conventions (2021), Gen. beliefs (2022), Equivariant ZSC (2022), ER symmetries (2025). Edges, 15 in total. Builds on (kind 0, 3): Other-Play -&gt; ZSC formalism, Other-Play -&gt; Equivariant ZSC, Other-Play -&gt; ER symmetries. Reuses or extends (kind 1, 6): Other-Play -&gt; OBL (setting, OP agents as baseline and partners), Other-Play -&gt; Gen. beliefs (SAD and OP policy pools; edge added by me, not in the citation graph), OBL -&gt; Gen. beliefs (codebase and belief idea), OBL -&gt; Equivariant ZSC (symmetrizer applied to OBL agents), Equivariant ZSC -&gt; ER symmetries (symmetrizer), ZSC formalism -&gt; ER symmetries (three-lever game, symmetrizer inspiration). Comparison or background only (kind 2, 6): Other-Play -&gt; Conventions, Conventions -&gt; Gen. beliefs, Conventions -&gt; Equivariant ZSC, Gen. beliefs -&gt; Equivariant ZSC, OBL -&gt; ER symmetries, Gen. beliefs -&gt; ER symmetries. Conventions has no solid edge: it joins by topic (conventions in cooperative play) and is cited as a contrast by two later members. Background from member notes: self-play (Tesauro 1994), SAD (Hu &amp; Foerster 2019), ad hoc teamwork (Stone et al.), cognitive hierarchies (Camerer et al.), Lewis 1969 conventions. The old tree t2 covered Other-Play, OBL, ZSC formalism, Equivariant ZSC and ER symmetries; its OBL -&gt; Other-Play link was baseline-only, here it is &#x27;reuses setting&#x27;. Numbers from different papers are not comparable.","Shared assumptions, with sources: cooperative shared reward and independent training with one meeting (Other-Play Sec. 2.2, 3; OBL Sec. 3; ZSC formalism Sec. 3-4). Symmetries given: Other-Play (Conclusion: &#x27;currently we are assuming the symmetries are given&#x27;), Equivariant ZSC (a subgroup of known symmetries), ZSC formalism (finding them is graph-isomorphism-complete); ER symmetries learns them from a policy pool at the cost of 2.25e9 Hanabi steps and k = 6 to 20 policies. Environment access: OBL (fictitious transitions; Equivariant ZSC&#x27;s related work says it requires simulator access, a belief model of its own hand, Sec. 4.6); Gen. beliefs (200 rollouts per legal move with pool policies). Partners: Equivariant ZSC states ZSC agents only need to do well with agents optimized for ZSC; Gen. beliefs states it improves cross-play against policies not pre-configured for it (tests 7 held-out SAD partners). Conventions assumes rational partners with known identities and tasks that differ only in reward (Sec. 3-5). Tie-breaking in ZSC formalism needs a shared arbitrary choice, which the authors themselves say conflicts with the spirit of ZSC. Humans: only Other-Play (20 club players); OBL uses Clone Bot as a proxy.","Symmetry branch: Other-Play trains against a randomly relabeled partner (expected return of agent 1 with a symmetry-permuted copy of agent 2). ZSC formalism shows OP can learn several incompatible optimal policies and keeps the one with the top shared hash score. Equivariant ZSC replaces data augmentation with a symmetrizer S(net) = average over g of relabel-back . net . relabel-input. ER symmetries learns return-preserving relabelings from a pool of self-play-optimal policies and uses them as the OP group. Belief branch: OBL uses a belief model of the hidden state under a fixed pi0 and fictitious transitions in which the partner plays pi1; level k+1 uses level k as pi0. Gen. beliefs trains an encoder-decoder Transformer (6 layers, 8 heads, d = 256) to predict the hidden cards autoregressively from rollouts of 6 pool policies, then uses it for Monte Carlo search (200 rollouts per move) or as input to a best response. Side line: Conventions multiplies a shared task module and one partner module per partner, with a regularizer weight lambda pulling the task module to the average response; PPO, 2-layer MLPs. Cross-links: Equivariant ZSC symmetrizes OBL level-5 agents; Gen. beliefs reuses OBL&#x27;s codebase and OP/SAD pools.","Symmetry branch: Other-Play trains against a randomly relabeled partner (expected return of agent 1 with a symmetry-permuted copy of agent 2). ZSC formalism shows OP can learn several incompatible optimal policies and keeps the one with the top shared hash score. Equivariant ZSC replaces data augmentation with a symmetrizer S(net) = average over g of relabel-back . net . relabel-input. ER symmetries learns return-preserving relabelings from a pool of self-play-optimal policies and uses them as the OP group. Belief branch: OBL uses a belief model of the hidden state under a fixed pi0 and fictitious transitions in which the partner plays pi1; level k+1 uses level k as pi0. Gen. beliefs trains an encoder-decoder Transformer (6 layers, 8 heads, d = 256) to predict the hidden cards autoregressively from rollouts of 6 pool policies, then uses it for Monte Carlo search (200 rollouts per move) or as input to a best response. Side line: Conventions multiplies a shared task module and one partner module per partner, with a regularizer weight lambda pulling the task module to the average response; PPO, 2-layer MLPs. Cross-links: Equivariant ZSC symmetrizes OBL level-5 agents; Gen. beliefs reuses OBL&#x27;s codebase and OP/SAD pools.","Paper: Hu, Lerer, Peysakhovich, Foerster; ICML 2020. Root of this tree. Outbound edges: ZSC formalism (builds on, 0), Equivariant ZSC (builds on, 0), ER symmetries (builds on, 0); OBL (reuses setting and agents as baseline, 1); Gen. beliefs (reuses SAD and OP policy pools, 1). Conventions only cites it as a contrast (comparison, 2). Numbers (Table 1, Hanabi 2 players, max 25, 12 models per method, cross-play = mean of non-diagonal entries): SAD 2.52, SAD+OP 15.32, SAD+AUX 17.65, SAD+AUX+OP 22.07. Self-play scores stay near 24 (23.97 to 24.09). Human study (Sec. 6.5): 20 board-game-club players, OP bot 15.75 (s.e.m. 1.23) vs self-play bot 9.15 (1.18); bomb-out 45% vs 85%; OP won 15 of 20 seed comparisons (p = 0.0041). Authors call the human result preliminary. Lever game: OP gets 0.9, self-play about 0.1 at test (text gives 0.11). Assumptions: Sec. 1, 2.2, 3, 6.2 and Conclusion: symmetries are given, finding them is future work; Hanabi color permutations only, so agents can still form &#x27;secretive conventions&#x27;; only 2 players tested. Caveat: which self-play variant the &#x27;SP bot&#x27; is is not stated beyond &#x27;SOTA self-play agent of Hu &amp; Foerster 2019&#x27;.","Paper: Hu, Lerer, Cui, Pineda, Brown, Foerster; ICML 2021. Edge in: Other-Play, kind 1 (reuses setting; OBL&#x27;s text frames ZSC as Hu et al. 2020&#x27;s setting and runs OP agents as baseline and as unseen partners &#x27;Rank Bot&#x27;; its method is different and needs no symmetries). The old tree recorded this as comparison only; I raised it to &#x27;reuses&#x27; because the problem setting is inherited. Edges out: Gen. beliefs (reuses codebase and belief idea, 1), Equivariant ZSC (applies symmetrizer to OBL level-5 agents, 1), ER symmetries (comparison only, 2: OBL fails the three-lever and cat/dog games there). Numbers (Table 1, 2-player Hanabi, 5 seeds, 5,000 games per model; columns SP / XP): SAD* 23.97 / 2.52; Other-play 24.14 / 21.77; K-Level 16.97 / 17.17; OBL L1 20.92 / 20.85; L2 23.41 / 23.24; L3 23.93 / 23.68; L4 24.10 / 23.76. With Clone Bot (human-data proxy): OBL 13.80 to 16.02 vs Other-play 8.55. 3 players (Section 6 text only): OBL L4 XP 23.02 vs OP 17.36. SAD numbers are not directly comparable (different architecture, per the paper). Caveats: OBL is below SAD and OP in same-run self-play at level 1 (20.92); the claim is about cross-play. No human study. Limits (Sec. 7): not extended to zero-sum or general-sum games. Needs a steppable simulator (my reading; Equivariant ZSC&#x27;s related work also says OBL requires simulator access).","Paper: Treutlein, Dennis, Oesterheld, Foerster; ICML 2021. Edge in: Other-Play, kind 0: formalizes other-play&#x27;s label-free setting and extends the algorithm with tie-breaking to fix its suboptimality. Edge out: ER symmetries (kind 1: its three-lever game is &#x27;inspired by Treutlein et al. (2021)&#x27; and its symmetrizer is &#x27;inspired by&#x27; it, ER symmetries Sec. 4.1 and 4.3). Numbers: all from Fig. 5 (no table). Optimum lines 0.5 (two-stage) and 2.0 (asymmetric) are exact axis values; others are read by eye, about +-0.03: two-stage OP (1 seed) about -0.03, 8+ seeds about 0.49 to 0.5; asymmetric 1 seed about 0.9, 4 seeds about 1.75, 32 seeds about 1.97. 320 policies per game, K in {2..32}, 20 hash seeds. Toy lever games only; no Hanabi. Caveats: proofs (Theorems 7, 8) are in appendices not in the PDF the notes were made from. The authors conclude tie-breaking is ultimately unsatisfactory as ZSC because it needs an arbitrary shared choice, and propose (but do not test) a new informal setting. Finding symmetries is graph-isomorphism-complete in general.","Paper: Shih, Sawhney, Kondic, Ermon, Sadigh; ICLR 2021. Edge in: Other-Play, kind 2 (comparison/background): the paper says that approach &#x27;avoids relying on conventions altogether; instead, the agents aim for unambiguous but potentially sub-optimal strategies&#x27; and contrasts its own aim of learning conventions. Edges out: Gen. beliefs and Equivariant ZSC cite it in related work (kind 2) as limited to bandit problems and 1-colour Hanabi. So this member joins the tree by topic, not by method. The tags file suggests it may fit better in a lane on inferring/adapting to partners (low confidence). Numbers (Fig. 7, Wasserstein distance between task module and oracle marginals, lower is better): Arms 3: lambda 0.5 = 0.01, lambda 0.3 = 0.01, lambda 0.0 = 0.46, BaselineAgg 1.15, BaselineAgg EarlyStopping 0.75. Arms 2: 0.02, 0.05, 0.61, 1.02, 0.74. Arms 4: 0.01, 0.04, 0.51, 1.00, 1.26. Blocks: 0.10, 0.15, 0.53, 0.44, 0.46. The Arms 2 lambda 0.5 label was paired by legend order. Not printed as numbers: adaptation curves (Figs. 6, 9, 11) and Fig. 10 zero-shot bars. Setup: bandit 4 contexts, 8 arms; blocks 2x2 grid, 6 turns; light Hanabi 1 colour, 5 ranks, 2 players. Human study: 23 pairs on the bandit. No seeds or statistical tests in the text read. Headline 0.01 vs 1.15 is Arms 3 only; the Blocks gap is 0.10 vs 0.44.","Paper: Muglich, Zintgraf, Schroeder de Witt, Whiteson, Foerster; ICML 2022. Edges in: OBL (kind 1: &#x27;We base our training setup on the codebases of Hu &amp; Foerster (2019); Hu et al. (2021b; 2020)&#x27;; OBL&#x27;s belief model is for one fixed policy, this paper trains one belief on many), Other-Play (kind 1: uses its 13 SAD policies and trains 12 OP policies as pools; this edge is not in the citation-matched graph, I added it after reading the text), Conventions (kind 2, related work). Edges out: none that reuse it; Equivariant ZSC and ER symmetries cite it (kind 2). Numbers (Table 1, mean +- s.e.m., 7 held-out SAD partners, max 25): BR without gen. belief 10.29 +- 1.05; with SBS 11.32; with GBS 12.01; BR with gen. belief 12.36 +- 0.96; with SBS 12.03; with GBS 12.47. OP partners (6 held out): BR 17.49 -&gt; 18.30 with gen. belief. SAD belief for OP partners 17.49 -&gt; 18.11, 18.99 with search. Significance (permutation tests): SAD p &lt;= 0.0000515; OP p about 0.058 to 0.063 (only at 0.1); SAD-for-OP p about 0.043 to 0.047. Adding search on top of BR with gen. belief gives no significant gain. Reference cross-play: SAD pool 6.258 +- 1.51, OP pool 16.88 +- 0.96. Caveats: only Hanabi; standard errors overlap between several rows; &#x27;new partners must resemble the pool&#x27; is my reading of Fig. 3 correlations, not an explicit assumption; belief quality (Fig. 2) read from the plot.","Paper: Muglich, Schroeder de Witt, van der Pol, Whiteson, Foerster; NeurIPS 2022. Edges in: Other-Play (kind 0: &#x27;Replaces Other-Play&#x27;s data-augmentation symmetry handling with equivariant networks; G-OP variant trains with sampled group permutations&#x27;); OBL (kind 1: it symmetrizes the 5 released OBL level-5 agents at test time and states that EQC &#x27;can improve on OBL, the state of the art for zero-shot coordination on the Hanabi benchmark at the time of writing&#x27;); Conventions and Gen. beliefs (kind 2: related work only). Edge out: ER symmetries (kind 1). Numbers: Table 1 (cross-play, 5000 games per pair, +- s.e.m.): OP (12 agents) 15.32 +- 0.65, C5-equivariant 15.45 +- 0.47, D10-equivariant 16.08 +- 0.42; bombout 30.8%, 24.3%, 19.7%. Table 2 (XP without / C5 / D10 symmetrizer): SAD 2.52 / 3.61 / 3.61; IQL 10.53 / 13.57 / 13.62; OP 15.32 / 16.07 / 16.48; OBL-L5 23.77 / 23.77 / 23.89 (p about 0.08 to 0.10). Caveats: the 16.08 vs 15.32 gap is within one standard error of each mean; no p-value stated for it. The bombout drop is the larger effect. Equivariance alone is not enough (a uniform random policy is equivariant). Hanabi player count not stated in main text; appendix not read.","Paper: Muglich, Forkel, van der Pol, Foerster; ICLR 2025. Edges in: Other-Play (kind 0: &#x27;Generalises Other-Play&#x27;s environment symmetries to expected-return symmetries, used as the symmetry group inside the OP objective&#x27;); Equivariant ZSC (kind 1: symmetrizer reused as test-time operator); ZSC formalism (kind 1: three-lever game &#x27;inspired by Treutlein et al. (2021)&#x27;, symmetrizer also inspired by it); OBL (kind 2: compared in related work and toy games where OBL fails, not used); Gen. beliefs (kind 2: cited as related work and Hanabi testbed). Numbers: Table 1, Hanabi (self-play / XP mean / XP median): IPPO 24.04 / 4.02 / 0.12; IPPO+OP with 120 Dec-POMDP symmetries 23.81 / 8.61 / 8.14; IPPO+OP with 11 ER symmetries 23.74 / 21.64 / 22.03. Overcooked V2 (one layout): cross-play mean 6.74 baseline vs 15.8 with ER symmetries, but self-play falls from 162.33 to 27.81. Search loop uses 2.25e9 Hanabi steps; pool of k = 6 to 20 policies. Caveats: the 8.61 for OP here uses IPPO, while Other-Play&#x27;s own paper reports 15.32 with SAD-based agents; not comparable. Meaning of the +- values undefined. Limit: relabelings of actions/observations only; the two-lever game still fails. OBL fails the three-lever game per the paper.","Headline numbers by paper, each from its own notes: Other-Play Table 1 (SAD 2.52, SAD+OP 15.32, Hanabi 2p, 12 models; 20 humans 15.75 vs 9.15); OBL Table 1 (L4 XP 23.76, OP 21.77); ZSC formalism Fig. 5 (two-stage lever game OP about 0 vs about 0.5, read by eye); Conventions Fig. 7 (Arms 3: 0.01 vs 1.15 distance to oracle marginals; Blocks 0.10 vs 0.44); Gen. beliefs Table 1 (7 SAD partners 10.29 -&gt; 12.36 +- 0.96; OP partners 17.49 -&gt; 18.30); Equivariant ZSC Tables 1-2 (D10 16.08 vs OP 15.32; bombouts 19.7% vs 30.8%); ER symmetries Table 1 (XP 21.64 vs 8.61, IPPO 4.02). Not comparable: ER symmetries&#x27; OP baseline (8.61, IPPO) and Other-Play&#x27;s own OP result (15.32, SAD) differ because the base algorithm differs. Gen. beliefs scores are best-response scores against SAD or OP partners, a different quantity from cross-play of independent agents. Conventions&#x27; number is a distance, not a game score. Gaps are measured against each paper&#x27;s own baseline. The human study is small (20, preliminary).","Headline numbers by paper, each from its own notes: Other-Play Table 1 (SAD 2.52, SAD+OP 15.32, Hanabi 2p, 12 models; 20 humans 15.75 vs 9.15); OBL Table 1 (L4 XP 23.76, OP 21.77); ZSC formalism Fig. 5 (two-stage lever game OP about 0 vs about 0.5, read by eye); Conventions Fig. 7 (Arms 3: 0.01 vs 1.15 distance to oracle marginals; Blocks 0.10 vs 0.44); Gen. beliefs Table 1 (7 SAD partners 10.29 -&gt; 12.36 +- 0.96; OP partners 17.49 -&gt; 18.30); Equivariant ZSC Tables 1-2 (D10 16.08 vs OP 15.32; bombouts 19.7% vs 30.8%); ER symmetries Table 1 (XP 21.64 vs 8.61, IPPO 4.02). Not comparable: ER symmetries&#x27; OP baseline (8.61, IPPO) and Other-Play&#x27;s own OP result (15.32, SAD) differ because the base algorithm differs. Gen. beliefs scores are best-response scores against SAD or OP partners, a different quantity from cross-play of independent agents. Conventions&#x27; number is a distance, not a game score. Gaps are measured against each paper&#x27;s own baseline. The human study is small (20, preliminary)."]});
